              "reasoning": "The finding flags a potential CSRF issue in a GET request handler ('do_GET'), but CSRF protection is only required for state-changing operations (POST, PUT, DELETE, etc.). This handler performs only read operations (SELECT queries, content rendering) and does not mutate server state. The INSERT into 'comments' is triggered by a GET request with a 'comment' parameter, which is a design flaw, but it is not a CSRF bypass \u2014 it is an improper use of HTTP semantics. The tool misclassified a state-changing GET as a CSRF vulnerability, when the real issue is the lack of proper HTTP method enforcement, not CSRF. No CSRF token or similar control is expected on GET endpoints, so the absence of CSRF protection here is not a vulnerability.",
